{ Welcome to the first study of the Vienna!

This study will introduce the so-called Vienna Gambit and answer the question: after
1.e4 e5
2.Nc3 Nf6
3.f4
What if Black simply takes our f-pawn?

White gets a fun and easy game! }
1. e4 e5 { Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels.
We develop our queenside knight.
This is slightly different than 2.Nf3, which is standard development. } 2. Nc3 Nf6 { In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!
We offer Black a capture of our f-pawn in order to push 4.e5 and attack the enemy knight... } 3. f4 exf4 { You will face this a shocking amount of times.
It simply lands Black in a dead lost position on move 3!
This is our plan:
4.e5
5.Nf3
6.d4
Finally, aim to capture the pawn back. } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Ge4e5,Gd2d4,Gg1f3] } 4. e5 Ng8 { Black decides to retreat the knight - this is the only safe square.
Black is now threatening Qh4+.
You MUST play Nf3 before you push d4, as this prevents Black's Qh4+. } { [%csl Re1,Rh4][%cal Rd8h4] } (4... Qe7 { Black plays 4...Qe7 and pins the e-pawn.
We continue by unpinning the pawn with 5.Qe2 - mirroring Black's move.
Now, Black has no choice - the knight must depart. } { [%csl Re5] } 5. Qe2 Ng8 { Black's knight retreats. Black is now threatening Qh4+.
We now develop and cover the check. } { [%csl Rh4][%cal Re7h4] } 6. Nf3 d6 { Black plays 6...d6 to develop the light-squared Bishop.
Let's play the knockout punch, punishing the queen for standing in front of the King. } { [%csl Rc7,Re7] } (6... Nc6 { Black might opt for 6...Nc6. } 7. d4 { We continue with 7.d4 and the threat of Nd5 is borderline unstoppable.
White has a winning advantage. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc3d5] }) 7. Nd5 Qd7 { Black's queen slides to safety, but... } 8. exd6+ { We chop down Black's position and win the game. } { [%csl Rc7,Re8][%cal Gd6c7] }) 5. Nf3 d6 { Black undermines your pawn, and prepares to develop his light-squared bishop.
We continue with 6.d4.
If Black captures in the center afterwards, our queen will become active. } (5... Nc6 { Black can also continue with 5...Nc6, a more flexible move.
We continue with d4 - expanding in the center and supporting the e-pawn. } { [%csl Re5] } 6. d4 d6 { Black undermines your pawn, and prepares to develop their light-squared Bishop.
We capture Black's f4-pawn. } { [%csl Re5,Gc8] } 7. Bxf4 dxe5 { Black captures our e-pawn. } 8. Qe2 { We continue with 8.Qe2.
We know this position. 8...Nxd4 9.Nxd4 and we will play Bxe5. } { [%csl Re5,Re8][%cal Gc6d4,Gf3d4,Gf4e5] }) 6. d4 dxe5 { Black captures the e-pawn.
We continue with 7.Qe2 - this is very important!
Now Black is frozen, you avoid a trade of queens, and you will recapture both center pawns before castling - preferably queenside. } { [%csl Re5] } 7. Qe2 Nc6 { 7...Nc6 is the most popular response at this point, but Black can do other things (we will review briefly in the video).
We take on f4 with the bishop. This is the important move, taking advantage of the pin. We also set up traps. } { [%csl Rd4,Rf4] } 8. Bxf4 Nxd4 { Black takes, threatening your queen.
We trade knights. } { [%cal Rd4e2] } 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 { Black recaptures on d4. } 10. Bxe5 { Success!
We capture Black's e-pawn and Black is going to lose everything. } { [%csl Gd4,Gg7,Re8][%cal Re5g7,Re5d4] } *


