{ In this study, we're continuing our analysis of the Vienna Gambit and taking a look at how to respond if Black declines our gambit with 3...Nc6. }
1. e4 e5 { Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels.
We develop our queenside knight. } 2. Nc3 Nf6 { In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit! } 3. f4 Nc6 { Believe it or not, defending the Vienna Gambit with 3...Nc6 is one of Black's most popular responses.
But, fortunately for us, this puts Black in a losing position!
You must take after Black plays 3...Nc6. } { [%csl Re5] } 4. fxe5 Nxe5 { Black recaptures on e5.
You have to push d4 to target the knights. } { [%csl Re5,Rf6] } 5. d4 Ng6 { This is 1 of 2 retreats available for Black.
Of the 2, this one is the better option for Black.
We push 6.e5 and attack Black's knight!
Black must retreat with ...Ng8 now. } (5... Nc6 { Of course, Black can go back this way too, but it's not quite effective.
We push 6.e5, advancing and attacking the knight. } { [%csl Rf6] } 6. e5 Ng8 { Black retreats the knight to g8.
We again cover Qh4+ and defend the center. 7.Nf3 Bb4 8.d5 is good for us. } { [%csl Rh4][%cal Rd8h4] } (6... Qe7 { A tricky response for Black would be to pin our e-pawn with 6... Qe7.
If we mirror the move with 7. Qe2 then Black can respond with 7... Nxd4. } { [%csl Re5,Re1] } 7. Be2 { Instead, we should simply respond with 7.Be2.
This unpins our e-pawn, and keeps our queen watching our d-pawn.
A reasonable move after this would be something like Nf3, defending the center. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gg1f3,Ge5f6] }) 7. Nf3 d6 { Black undermines the e-pawn, and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop.
We continue by pinning Black's knight with 8.Bb5. } 8. Bb5 dxe5 { Black captures the e-pawn.
We recapture with the knight, pressuring the c6-knight. } { [%csl Rc6] } 9. Nxe5 Bd7 { Black supports the c6-knight with 9...Bd7.
We will capture the bishop with 10.Nxd7. } 10. Nxd7 Qxd7 { Black recaptures on d7 and is ready to castle queenside.
Let's make sure they don't get the time. } { [%cal Ge8c8] } 11. d5 { We continue with 11.d5, creating tactics in our favor.
11...Qe7+ 12.Qe2 a6 13.dxc6 and White comes out on top because we can take on b7 at the end of many variations. } { [%csl Rb7][%cal Gd5c6,Ge1g1,Gc6b7] }) 6. e5 Ng8 { Black retreats the knight to g8.
Nf3 is a flexible developing move and it prevents an annoying Qh4+. } { [%csl Rh4][%cal Rd8h4] } 7. Nf3 d6 { 7...d6 is Black's most direct attempt at fighting back against the center.
We don't need to be afraid - we develop the light-squared bishop and target Black's king. } { [%csl Re5] } 8. Bc4 dxe5 { Black captures the e-pawn.
We simply castle.
We now have the entire kingside developed, and we're ready to strike. } { [%csl Rf7] } 9. O-O exd4 { Black is greedy and captures the d-pawn. } { [%csl Rf7] } 10. Ng5 { We continue with 10.Ng5.
There are unstoppable intentions on f7. White is completely winning! } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gg5f7] } *


