{ In this study, we're continuing our analysis of the Vienna Gambit and taking a look at how to respond if Black declines our gambit with 3...d6. }
1. e4 e5 { Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels.
We develop our queenside knight. } 2. Nc3 Nf6 { In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit! } 3. f4 d6 { Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common.
We will simply develop our knight. } { [%csl Re5] } 4. Nf3 Nc6 { Black develops with 4...Nc6, supporting the center - this is most common.
We will pin the c6 knight to make the e-pawn a target. } { [%csl Rc6] } (4... exf4 { Black captures on f4, but there is nothing to worry about here.
We continue with 5.d4 - grabbing central space and opening up an attack on Black's f4-pawn. } { [%csl Rf4] } 5. d4 Qe7 { Black plays 5...Qe7 - counterattacking our center.
Don't rush, respect the opponent's threats. } { [%csl Re4] } (5... Nh5 { Black can also try to defend the pawn with 5...Nh5.
We respond 6.Nd5 - attacking the f4 pawn once more. } { [%csl Gf4] } 6. Nd5 g5 { Black defends the f-pawn with 6...g5, but this is a mistake!
We sacrifice the knight on g5! } { [%csl Gf4] } 7. Nxg5 Qxg5 { Black captures on g5.
We can capture Black's knight on h5, temporarily sacrificing our queen! } { [%csl Rf6,Rh5][%cal Gd5f6] } 8. Qxh5 Qxh5 { Black recaptures on h5. } { [%csl Re8,Rh5] } 9. Nf6+ { We continue with 9.Nf6+, forking Black's king and queen!
Black's king is now forced to move. Soon we will capture the queen and win the f4-pawn.
Black's position is terrible and White has a winning advantage. } { [%csl Gh5,Ge8,Gf4][%cal Gf6e8,Gf6h5] }) 6. Bd3 { We defend the e-pawn with 6.Bd3.
White is better, and we will simply continue by capturing on f4. } { [%csl Ge4,Rf4][%cal Gc1f4] }) (4... Bg4 { Black can also pin our knight with 4...Bg4 - this is actually the second most common move.
We immediately attack the bishop with 5.h3. } { [%csl Rf3] } 5. h3 Bxf3 { Black captures on f3.
We recapture with the queen. } (5... Bh5 { If Black retreats the bishop to h5, we can trap it with g4 + f5. } { [%cal Gg2g4,Gf4f5] }) 6. Qxf3 Nc6 { Black develops with 6...Nc6, supporting the center.
We continue with 7.Bb5. } { [%csl Ge5] } (6... exf4 { If Black takes on f4, we can play d4!
Bxf4 will follow, and White has a position with total central control. 0-0 will also add a rook to the semi-open f-file. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4,Gf1c4,Ge1g1] }) (6... Be7 { If Black opts to develop with 6...Be7, we can continue developing with Bc4 + O-O + d3. } { [%csl Gd3,Gc4][%cal Gd2d3,Gf1c4,Ge1g1] }) 7. Bb5 { Our main objectives are to undermine e5. We will take on c6 and double the pawns, and then open up the f-file. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Rf4e5,Rb5c6] }) 5. Bb5 Bd7 { Black unpins the knight with 5...Bd7.
We continue with 6.d3.
Now we are ready to capture on c6, followed by e5. } (5... Bg4 { Sometimes, this pin occurs.
We should attack the bishop with 6.h3. } { [%csl Rf3] } 6. h3 Bxf3 { Black captures on f3.
We recapture with the queen. } 7. Qxf3 Be7 { Black develops with 7...Be7 and prepares O-O.
I recommend a very simple transformation of the position.
We capture on c6 - giving up the bishop pair, but ruining Black's pawn structure. } 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Black recaptures on c6.
We capture on e5. } { [%csl Rc7,Rc6] } 9. fxe5 dxe5 { Black recaptures and now their pawn structure is in ruins.
We shall castle, push d3, and have a calm advantage. } { [%csl Rc7,Rc6,Re5] } 10. O-O O-O { Black also castles their king. } 11. d3 { We continue 11.d3, with a pleasant position.
We will develop the dark-squared bishop and attempt to target Black's weaknesses.
If Black activates their rook with ...Rb8, don't play b3!
Instead, we can support the pawn with Nd1 or Rb1. } { [%csl Ge3,Gg5][%cal Gc1e3,Ge3g5] }) 6. d3 exf4 { Black releases the tension and captures on f4. } (6... Nd4 { Black also has the option to jump the knight onto d4. The following line is pretty forced.
We capture the bishop on d7 with check. } { [%csl Rb5,Rf3] } 7. Bxd7+ Qxd7 { Black recaptures with the queen.
We capture the knight on d4. } 8. Nxd4 exd4 { Black recaptures on d4 and attacks our knight.
We retreat our knight to e2 and attack the d-pawn. } { [%csl Rc3] } 9. Ne2 c5 { Black defends the pawn with 9...c5. } { [%csl Gd4] } 10. O-O { We castle our king.
We have a pretty comfortable position. If Black castles kingside some of our ideas include Ng3, Bd2 and f5.
If Black castles queenside, some of our attacking ideas include a4 + b4 + Rb1, but we can also attack Black's center with c3. } { [%csl Gd2,Gg3,Gf5,Re7,Rg8][%cal Gf4f5,Ge2g3,Gc1d2,Rf8e7,Re8g8] }) 7. Bxf4 { We recapture with the bishop.
Our position is very comfortable and we will continue with Qd2 or Qe2, followed by castling kingside or queenside. } { [%csl Gd2,Ge2,Gc1,Gg1][%cal Gd1d2,Gd1e2,Ge1c1,Ge1g1] } *


