{ We have to study what can happen if Black deviates from theory on move 2!

We will begin with the move 2...d6, a slow pawn move that could be played. }
1. e4 e5 { Black will mirror us.
We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna.
And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or d6!
As 2...d6 is our subject, we'll see that in this study. } 2. Nc3 d6 { This is definitely a passive setup, but it's completely possible.
We develop the bishop first, before going for the d3/f4 setup. } 3. Bc4 Nf6 { This is the most popular move. Black develops a knight.
4.d3 protects the e4 pawn and opens up the dark-squared bishop.
5.f4 will be our next move. } { [%csl Ge4,Gc1] } (3... c6 { For some reason, this move is the second most popular on the lichess player database.
d3 and f4 is our plan. Without a knight on c6, Black is less equipped to deal with pressure on e5. } 4. d3 Nf6 { The knight is brought out to support d5.
We push. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Gd6d5] } (4... b5 { If Black goes for b5 ideas, we bring the bishop backwards. } { [%csl Rc4] } 5. Bb3 a5 { And if they threaten to trap your bishop... } { [%cal Ra5a4] } 6. a4 { Just don't let that happen!
Our idea is to play f4 and Nf3. If 6...b4 7.Nce2 is solid for White. } { [%cal Gb5b4,Gc3e2,Gf2f4,Gg1f3] }) 5. f4 exf4 { Black resolves the tension before pushing d5.
We recapture. } { [%csl Rf4] } 6. Bxf4 d5 { ...d5 is achieved, but we can simply trade. } { [%csl Re4,Rc4] } 7. exd5 cxd5 { Black takes.
Let's bring the bishop to b3. } { [%csl Rc4] } 8. Bb3 d4 { And if d4, we first insert a check. } { [%csl Rc3,Re8] } 9. Qe2+ Be7 { And once the bishop blocks... } 10. Ne4 { We can get our knight out of danger and castle afterwards. } { [%cal Gg1f3,Ge1g1] }) (3... f5 { Some players are crazy like this!
They go for ...f5 to undermine the center.
Let's just take. Did Black really think this through? } { [%csl Re4] } 4. exf5 Bxf5 { Black here usually recaptures.
But now we have a plan: Nf3, 0-0 and d4! } 5. Nf3 Nf6 { Thus after Black develops, we simply continue with our plan of 0-0. } 6. O-O Nc6 { Black develops the knight, and so here comes d4!
This is actually very strong: if massive exchanges occur on e5, Black would have an isolated pawn! } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Rd4e5] } 7. d4 exd4 { Just to exemplify how badly things can go, we can give a check here. } { [%csl Re8] } 8. Re1+ Be7 { If the bishop blocks, Nd5 adds an attacker... } { [%csl Re7] } 9. Nd5 Nxd5 { Black is compelled to take, and logically we recapture. } 10. Bxd5 Kf8 { Black is advised to break the pin because Bg5 was coming!
Let's remove d4's defender. } { [%csl Rc6,Rd4] } 11. Bxc6 bxc6 { And after Black takes... } 12. Nxd4 { The knight jumps into the center of the board! White is winning. } { [%csl Rc6,Rf5][%cal Gd4c6] }) 4. d3 Be7 { Black usually continues with normal development.
Let's play f4! } { [%csl Re5] } 5. f4 exf4 { It's not unusual for Black to take.
We just recapture, that's why our bishop is there! } { [%csl Rf4] } (5... O-O { Sometimes, Black just castles.
Let's add pressure on e5 with our knight. } { [%csl Re5] } 6. Nf3 Nc6 { Black defends.
And here we can go f5! } { [%csl Ge5] } 7. f5 Na5 { Black usually goes for the bishop, as they should.
If they don't do this, feel free to preserve your bishop with a3 ideas.
And here I suggest we develop the other bishop. } { [%csl Rc4] } 8. Bg5 Nxc4 { The bishop is taken... } 9. dxc4 { We recapture. We're going to bring our queen up and castle queenside to attack on the kingside. } { [%cal Gd1d3,Ge1c1] }) 6. Bxf4 O-O { Black castles.
And now we continue developing our pieces, getting ready to castle. } 7. Nf3 Bg4 { If Black goes for this very popular move, just kick them out with h3. If Bxf3, Qxf3, and you have the bishop pair. } { [%csl Rf3] } 8. h3 Bh5 { The bishop goes back, so we attack it once more. } { [%csl Rh5] } 9. g4 Bg6 { It goes back.
We now develop the queen. } 10. Qd2 { Now, in this particular position, 0-0-0 is more logical! The attack has been set up on the kingside! } { [%cal Ge1c1] } *


