{ We will now see 2...Bc5 and 2...Bb4, the bishop moves, as early side lines!

We can still play in a Vienna Style and get a solid position. }
1. e4 e5 { Black will mirror us.
We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna.
And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6! } 2. Nc3 Bc5 { Black can choose to develop the bishop first, without committing the knight yet.
Here I suggest we go for the immediate f4.
If Black takes, you'll notice there's no knight on f6 to harass with e5. } { [%csl Re5] } (2... Bb4 { Black could develop their bishop to b4.
An idea could be to trade off the bishop before playing d6 and going for a dark square color complex.
Let's just expand in the center and play 3.d4! } { [%csl Rc3,Re5] } 3. d4 Bxc3+ { Taking is very natural for Black.
We can just recapture. } (3... exd4 { Black could also just take in the center right away, they often do this.
We recapture, forking bishop and pawn. } { [%csl Rb4,Rg7] } 4. Qxd4 Bxc3+ { That's why many players just take.
We recapture on c3. } 5. Qxc3 Nf6 { And again, we have a pawn in the center and Black has none.
We can just develop our bishop to castle queenside soon.
The knight can't take on e4 because we would just take on g7. } { [%csl Re4,Rg7] } 6. Be3 Qe7 { So maybe now the queen can be developed to attack e4.
We can defend with f3. Black's best plan would then be ...Nc6 + ...Qb4 for a queen trade. } { [%csl Ge4] } 7. f3 Nc6 { So suppose they play Nc6 which is a developing move... } 8. O-O-O { Just castle queenside, and you'll stand much better.
Even if Black does find the idea of trading queens, we can just go ahead and trade and develop our bishops which control many squares. }) (3... d6 { ...d6, defending e5 now that the bishop is out of the pawn chain, is also natural.
We will simply trade pawns on e5. } { [%csl Ge5] } 4. dxe5 dxe5 { Black recaptures.
Now we trade queens and make Black lose castling rights. } { [%csl Rd8,Rd1] } (4... Bxc3+ { If Black chooses to interject this move, we can just recapture. Now we have the bishop pair. } { [%csl Re1] } 5. bxc3 dxe5 { Black takes in the center.
We trade queens. } { [%csl Rd8,Rd1] } 6. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { Black recaptures.
We play Bc4. Things can go wrong for Black quickly. } { [%csl Rf7] } 7. Bc4 f6 { They can defend, but our bishops are coming to life.
This is the bishop pair advantage!
We open up lines with f4. } { [%csl Re5] } 8. f4 exf4 { If they take, sure they have a better structure. } { [%csl Rf4] } 9. Bxf4 { But our piece activity is just insane. White is much better.
We'll play 0-0-0+, Nf3, and our idea will be to get the e5-pawn push to get rid of our isolated e-pawn.
If Black ever plays ...Bg4, h3 will force it off the diagonal. } { [%csl Rc7,Rd8][%cal Gg1f3,Ge1c1] }) 5. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { They recapture.
We defend our knight. } { [%csl Gc3] } 6. Bd2 Nf6 { Black adds pressure on e4.
But we can castle and defend e4 indirectly. } { [%csl Re4,Rd8] } 7. O-O-O Ke7 { The king gets out of the way.
And now we defend e4 with our rook. } { [%csl Ge4] } 8. Re1 Rd8 { If the black rook enters the open d-file, we can actually get the bishop out of the diagonal starting with a3. } { [%csl Rb4] } 9. a3 Bd6 { And once they move up... } 10. f4 { f4 is quite strong, and puts pressure on the Black position!
If 10...exf4, 11.e5! We've got sound developing moves like Nf3 and Bc4 coming. } { [%cal Gf1c4,Gg1f3,Re4e5,Ge5f4] }) (3... Nc6 { The simple 3...Nc6 is also not optimal.
We can just take in the center.
Now if Black throws in Bxc3+, we'll just have the bishop pair in an open position. } { [%csl Rd4] } 4. dxe5 Nxe5 { The most popular move is recapturing.
So now we can fork both pieces. } { [%csl Re5,Rb4] } 5. Qd4 Bxc3+ { And now after Black captures, we recapture with the queen, with pressure on the knight. } { [%csl Rc3] } 6. Qxc3 d6 { And after these very natural moves, White has an advantage.
We can just develop and attack e5. } 7. Nf3 Nxf3+ { And let's say Black chooses the very natural trade here... } 8. gxf3 { Who's got the stronger center and the better prospect for their pieces?
Certainly White.
We have the bishop pair advantage and pressure on the g7 and c7 pawns. } { [%csl Gf3,Ge4,Rg7,Rc7][%cal Gh1g1] }) 4. bxc3 exd4 { Now Black takes in the center once again.
But this just leaves us with a strong center! } 5. cxd4 d6 { d6 comes a little too late now, after Black has improved our position with all those bad trades.
We can just develop normally and stand much better. } { [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } 6. Bd3 Ne7 { For example, Black develops, and we develop our knight, getting ready to castle. } 7. Ne2 O-O { Black castles... } 8. O-O { And so do we.
Black needs counterplay with c5 fast, in which case we'll just go c3.
Our center is really strong. } { [%cal Gc2c3,Gc7c5] }) 3. f4 exf4 { So if Black takes, I advise you to develop the knight. } (3... d6 { Black could choose to defend the pawn.
We develop the knight, attacking e5. } { [%csl Ge5] } 4. Nf3 Nc6 { Black responds by defending with the knight.
Let's just go for the bishop with Na4. } { [%csl Ge5] } (4... Nf6 { 4...Nf6 is also possible.
